---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { paintingBySlug } from '../../../lib/content';
import { STUDIES, PERIOD, selfSimilarity } from '../../../lib/droste';

const studies = STUDIES.map(paintingBySlug);
const { scale, turn } = selfSimilarity();
const PAPER = 'https://www.ams.org/notices/200304/fea-escher.pdf';
const POSTERX = 'https://posterxclub.com/escher';
---

<Base
  title="Print Gallery, without end"
  description="Escher's Print Gallery left a hole in the middle. Two mathematicians worked out how to fill it. Zoom forever into the same twist, applied to four paintings from this museum."
  space="per"
>
  <p class="crumb"><a href="/personal/art/">Art and myth</a> · Annex, the picture inside itself</p>
  <header class="head">
    <h1>Print Gallery, without end</h1>
    <p class="by">After M. C. Escher, <cite>Prentententoonstelling</cite> (Print Gallery), 1956, and B. de Smit and H. W. Lenstra Jr., 2003</p>
  </header>

  <figure class="exhibit">
    <div class="stage">
      <canvas
        data-droste
        data-studies={studies.map(p => p.image.src).join(' ')}
        tabindex="0"
        role="img"
        aria-label={`An endless spiral zoom. ${studies.map(p => p.title).join(', ')} hang one inside the next, twisted the way Escher twisted his print.`}
        aria-describedby="zoom-help"
        hidden></canvas>
      <div class="fallback" data-fallback>
        <span class="nest" aria-hidden="true">
          {studies.slice(0, 3).map(p => <img src={p.image.thumb} width={p.image.thumbWidth} height={p.image.thumbHeight} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />)}
        </span>
        <p>The zoom needs JavaScript and WebGL 2. Without them, this is the straight picture: each painting hangs inside the one before, a quarter of its size.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="zoom-controls" data-controls hidden>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-zoom="-0.25">Zoom out</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-zoom="0.25">Zoom in</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-play aria-pressed="false">Play</button>
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-twist aria-pressed="true">Escher's twist</button>
      <output class="meta" data-out>0 repeats in</output>
    </div>
    <figcaption id="zoom-help">
      Scroll, drag up or down, or use the buttons. With the picture focused, + and − zoom and P plays or pauses. Turn off
      “Escher's twist” to see the straight picture he started from. The paintings are cropped to squares.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="hole">
    <h2 id="hole">The hole in the middle</h2>
    <p>
      In 1956 Escher made a lithograph of a young man in an exhibition gallery, looking at a print of a Mediterranean
      seaport. As his eyes follow the buildings on the quay from left to right and then down, he finds among them the
      gallery he is standing in. In the middle of the print is a round white patch with Escher's monogram and signature.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="method">
    <h2 id="method">Escher's method: four studies and a curved grid</h2>
    <p>
      Escher wanted “a cyclic expansion … without beginning or end”. He tried straight lines first, then curved ones, and
      ended with a grid whose squares grow by 4 in each direction from one corner to the next. Going once clockwise round
      the centre, the grid folds back onto itself, expanded by 4<sup>4</sup> = {PERIOD}.
    </p>
    <p>
      He also drew an ordinary, undistorted scene: the gallery, the print, the seaport, and the gallery again, {PERIOD}
      times smaller. To keep enough detail at every size he made four studies, each showing part of the one before,
      blown up by 4. Then he copied the straight squares of the studies onto the curved grid, square by square.
    </p>
    <p>
      The zoom above does the same with four paintings from this museum, one from each room: each hangs inside the one
      before at a quarter of its size, so after four the picture repeats at {PERIOD} times smaller. Turn off the twist to
      see these straight studies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="maths">
    <h2 id="maths">What the mathematicians found</h2>
    <p>
      Bart de Smit and Hendrik Lenstra, at Leiden, treated the picture as drawn on the complex plane. The straight
      picture <var>f</var> repeats when you scale it by {PERIOD}: <var>f</var>(256<var>z</var>) = <var>f</var>(<var>z</var>).
      They showed that Escher's distorted picture can be seen as drawn on an elliptic curve over the complex numbers, and
      that his grid is, almost exactly, the map
    </p>
    <p class="formula">
      <var>h</var>(<var>w</var>) = <var>w</var><sup><var>α</var></sup>, with <var>α</var> = (2π<var>i</var> + log 256) / (2π<var>i</var>)
    </p>
    <p>
      One loop round the centre of the finished print has to land one loop round and {PERIOD} times larger in the
      straight picture; that one condition fixes <var>α</var>. It follows that an idealised Print Gallery contains a copy
      of itself, turned clockwise by about {turn.toFixed(1)} degrees and shrunk by a factor of about {scale.toFixed(2)}.
      Both numbers on this page are computed from the formula when the site is built.
    </p>
    <p>
      Their central square comes out smaller than Escher's, because 22.58 is larger than the factor measured in his
      grid. Escher had stopped short of the middle; the formula tells you what goes there.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="filled">
    <h2 id="filled">Filling it in</h2>
    <p>
      To fill the hole, they first rebuilt Escher's straight studies from the grid and the lithograph by running his
      procedure backwards, with software written by Joost Batenburg, then a mathematics student at Leiden. The blank spot
      became an empty spiral. The Dutch artists Hans Richter and Jacqueline Hofstra completed and adjusted the drawings,
      and Hofstra added the grey tones. Mapping the straight picture through <var>h</var> then gave the completed print.
    </p>
    <p>
      Escher's own picture and the Leiden completion are not shown here: Escher's work is in copyright, held by the M.C.
      Escher Company. Everything you see in the zoom is a public-domain painting from this museum, bent by the same
      formula in your browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="part" aria-labelledby="credits">
    <h2 id="credits">Sources and credit</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        B. de Smit and H. W. Lenstra Jr., <a href={PAPER}>“The Mathematical Structure of Escher's Print Gallery”</a>,
        <cite>Notices of the AMS</cite> 50(4), April 2003, pp. 446–451. The facts, quotations and numbers on this page
        come from it; the Escher quotations are from Bruno Ernst, <cite>The Magic Mirror of M. C. Escher</cite> (1976), as
        quoted there.
      </li>
      <li>
        The idea for this exhibit comes from <a href={POSTERX}>poster×club's zoom into the Leiden Print Gallery</a>,
        which shows Escher's picture itself. Go there to see the real thing.
      </li>
      <li>Paintings: credits are on each painting's page, linked from <a href="/personal/art/">the museum</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<script src="../../../scripts/droste.ts"></script>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-n1);
  }
  .head h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font: var(--text-5) / 1.05 var(--font-display);
  }
  .head .by {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-1);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }
  .exhibit {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
  }
  .stage {
    position: relative;
    width: min(100%, 720px);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    background: #120c14;
    border: 3px solid #b8893a;
    box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 2px #6e4f22;
  }
  canvas {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    touch-action: none;
    cursor: ns-resize;
  }
  canvas:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .fallback {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    align-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .fallback p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 44ch;
    color: #f1e7ef;
  }
  .nest {
    position: relative;
    width: 60%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
  }
  .nest img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    margin: auto;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    outline: 3px solid #b8893a;
  }
  .nest img:nth-child(2) {
    width: 25%;
    height: 25%;
  }
  .nest img:nth-child(3) {
    width: 6.25%;
    height: 6.25%;
  }
  .zoom-controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  .zoom-controls .btn[aria-pressed='true'] {
    background: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-canvas);
  }
  .zoom-controls output {
    margin-left: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    max-width: 70ch;
    font-size: var(--text-n1);
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }
  .part {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .part h2 {
    font: var(--text-2) / 1.2 var(--font-display);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .formula {
    font: var(--text-1) / 1.5 var(--font-display);
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    text-align: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
